<title>DENMARK: DANISH SUGAR BEET SEEN OVERCOMING LACK OF WATER.</title>
<headline>DANISH SUGAR BEET SEEN OVERCOMING LACK OF WATER.</headline>
<dateline>COPENHAGEN 1997-08-11</dateline>
<text>
<p>Denmark's sugar beet crop is lacking moisture amid the current heatwave, but prospects for a good harvest are not threatened, the Danish Department of Plant Production told Reuters on Monday.</p>
<p>&quot;The heatwave we have been having in Denmark in recent weeks means that sugar beets lack water and have shrivelled to preserve what moisture they can,&quot; a department spokesman said.</p>
<p>He said this did not necessarily threaten the harvest, due in early October, as precipitation would in all likelihood revive the beet by that time.</p>
<p>Danisco, Europe's fourth biggest sugar producer with a virtual monopoly of production in Denmark and Sweden and with eastern German plantations, is to announce the first sugar test results of the new season on or around August 13.</p>
<p>--Chris Follett, Copenhagen newsroom +45-33969652</p>
